"161201","Malik said that Yahya ibn Said had related to him that A'isha said that Amir ibn Fuhayra had said at the time of the epidemic"
"161202","Malik said that Yahya ibn Said had related to him that A'isha said that Amir ibn Fuhayra had said at the time of the epidemic"
"161301","Malik narrated to me that it reached him that 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz judged concerning a mudabbar (slave promised manumission upon death) who wounded someone: his master may hand over what he owns of him to the wounded person, who then employs him and deducts his wages from the blood money of the wound. If he pays it off before his master dies, he returns to his master. Malik said: The ruling with us concerning a mudabbar who wounds then his master dies, leaving no other property, is that one-third of him is manumitted, then the blood money of the wound is divided into thirds. One-third of the blood money is upon the third that was manumitted, and two-thirds upon the two-thirds in the hands of the heirs. If they wish, they hand over their portion of him to the wounded person; if they wish, they pay two-thirds of the blood money and retain their share of the slave. This is because the liability of that wound originated from the slave, not as a debt upon the master, so it does not invalidate the master's manumission and tadbir. If the master has debts to people along with the slave's offense, the mudabbar is sold to cover the blood money and the debts. The blood money from the slave's offense is paid first from the slave's price, then the master's debts, then whatever remains of the slave is examined: one-third is manumitted, two-thirds remain for the heirs. This is because the slave's offense has priority over the master's debt, and the master's debt has priority over the tadbir, which is only a bequest in one-third of the deceased's wealth. It should not be allowed that any part of the tadbir takes effect while the master's debt remains unpaid, for it is a bequest. Allah says: {After a bequest or a debt} (4:11). Malik said: If the deceased's one-third suffices to manumit the mudabbar entirely, he is manumitted, and the blood money of his offense becomes a debt upon him to be pursued after his manumission, even if it is the full diya, provided the master has no other debts. Malik said concerning a mudabbar who wounds a man, and his master hands him over to the wounded person, then the master dies with debts and no other property: the heirs say, ""We will hand him over to the wounded person."" The creditor says, ""I will pay more than that."" If the creditor increases the offer, he has priority, and the amount he increases over the blood money is deducted from the debt. If he does not increase, he does not take the slave. Malik said concerning a mudabbar who wounds and has property, but his master refuses to ransom him: the wounded person takes the mudabbar's property for the blood money. If it suffices, he takes the full blood money and returns the mudabbar to his master. If it does not suffice, he takes it from the blood money and employs the mudabbar for the remainder."
"161302","Yahya narrated to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from 'Abd al-Hamid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab set out for Sham. When he reached Sargh, the commanders of the troops met him: Abu 'Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah and his companions. They informed him that the plague had broken out in the land of Sham. Ibn 'Abbas said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab said, ""Call the early Muhajirun to me."" They were called, he consulted them, and informed them of the plague in Sham. They differed: some said, ""You set out for a purpose, and we do not see that you should return from it."" Others said, ""With you are the remnants of the people and the companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and we do not see that you should expose them to this plague."" 'Umar said, ""Rise away from me."" Then he said, ""Call the Ansar to me."" They were called, he consulted them, and they followed the way of the Muhajirun and differed as they differed. He said, ""Rise away from me."" Then he said, ""Call those here of the elders of Quraysh from the Muhajirun of the Conquest."" They were called, and not two of them differed: they said, ""We see that you should return with the people and not expose them to this plague."" 'Umar called out to the people: ""I am setting out in the morning, so set out with me."" Abu 'Ubaydah said: ""Are you fleeing from the decree of Allah?"" 'Umar said: ""Would that someone other than you said it, O Abu 'Ubaydah. Yes, we flee from the decree of Allah to the decree of Allah. What do you think? If you had camels and they went down a valley with two slopes, one fertile and the other barren, would you not be grazing the fertile by the decree of Allah, and the barren by the decree of Allah?"" Then 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf arrived — he had been absent for some need — and said: ""I have knowledge of this. I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'If you hear of it in a land, do not enter it; and if it breaks out in a land where you are, do not leave fleeing from it.'"" 'Umar praised Allah, then departed. Muwatta Imam Malik, Hadith 1613."
"161401","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ismail ibn Abi Hakim that he heard Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz say"
"161402","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ismail ibn Abi Hakim that he heard Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz say"
"161501","Malik related to me from Nafi that Abdullah ibn Umar said, ""The Jews came to the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, and mentioned to him that a man and woman from among them had committed adultery. The Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, asked them, 'What do you find in the Torah about stoning?' They said, 'We make their wrong action known and flog them.' Abdullah ibn Salam said, 'You have lied! It has stoning for it, so bring the Torah.' They spread it out and one of them placed his hand over the ayat of stoning. Then he read what was before it and what was after it. Abdullah ibn Salam told him to lift his hand. He lifted his hand and there was the ayat of stoning. They said, 'He has spoken the truth, Muhammad. The ayat of stoning is in it.' So the Messenger of Allah, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, gave the order and they were stoned . "" Abdullah ibn Umar added, ""I saw the man leaning over the woman to protect her from the stones."" Malik commented, ""By leaning he meant throwing himself over her so that the stones fell on him."""""""
